{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Final report — closed, landed as f05077afa (Gerrit 1995)\n\n**Problem.** `rebar transition <child> closed in_progress` did not cascade to a closed\nparent, leaving a closed parent holding an `in_progress` child.\n\n**Proven root cause.** `_CASCADING_EDGES` (`src/rebar/_commands/transition.py`) lacked the\n`(\"closed\", \"in_progress\")` key; `_cascade_parent_first` returns immediately on a lookup miss,\nso the cascade was a no-op on that edge alone.\n\n**Proof.** Reproduced live (exit 0, `child=in_progress parent=closed`). The discriminating\nexperiment: on the SAME store and fixture, `closed -> open` DOES cascade while\n`closed -> in_progress` does not — same helper, same parent link, one variable. That kills\n\"parent resolution broken\", \"fixture parent missing\", and \"cascade globally off\".\n\n**Is the state genuinely invalid — yes, four citations.** `docs/concurrency.md` I4a: \"A child\nnever runs ahead of its parent in the lifecycle\"; the same section names\nclosed-parent-with-open-child \"an invalid ... state\" outright; `_lib_writes.py` `reopen`\npromises no \"closed parent holding a non-closed child\"; and the close path's\nunresolved-children guard refuses to compose the state from the other direction.\n\n**What `reopen` does by comparison.** `reopen` is `transition(id, \"closed\", \"open\")`, an edge\nthat IS in the table, so the sanctioned closed-to-active path already cascades with fail-fast,\ncycle guard and TOCTOU recheck. The direct reactivation edge was the outlier beside an\nalready-closed hole — all the machinery existed.\n\n**Restoration, not widening.** `closed -> in_progress` already succeeded before this change;\nnothing previously refused becomes permitted, and no entry point, artifact, dependency or\nmutating call was added. **The change can only NARROW:** the cascaded parent runs its own\nstart-work plan-review gate, so some child reactivations that succeeded before are now refused\nwith an error naming the parent — the behaviour `open -> in_progress` has had since the gate\nwas keyed on target status. A later reader should not mistake this for loosening a lifecycle\nrule; it tightens one.\n\n**Test.** `tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_claim_transition_parent_cascade.py` (8 cases) and\n`tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_plan_review_gate.py` (1 gate case). Seen RED first — 5 failed\nfor the right reason, 4 passed trivially — then held OUT of the fix subagent's tree, and GREEN\nafterwards against an oracle it never saw.\n\n**Kill matrix — no survivors.** Deleting the one table entry returned all 5 to RED; restored\nand re-verified. Refactoring litmus passes: every assertion is on observable state (status via\n`show_ticket`, exit codes, error text), never a private name or a source grep.\n\n**Fix.** One dict key. Every existing semantic — recursion, cycle guard, fail-fast naming the\nparent, `ConcurrencyMismatch` identity at the leaf, the TOCTOU benign-race recheck,\n`cascade=False` suppression — came along untouched because they are already driven by that\ntable. That is the strongest evidence the scope is right.\n\n**Doc correction, attributed accurately.** `docs/ticket-model.md` claimed only\n`open -> in_progress` cascades. That was **already false before this commit** — stale since the\n`closed -> open` reopen edge landed in `ed864a9726` — so it is a pre-existing error corrected in\npassing, not collateral from this change.\n\n**Origin — `caused_by` a392-f630-7b25-4b14 (cranial-sulfur-peafowl), class `plan_defect`.**\nBy scope-of-work, and the blame trail agrees rather than diverging: `git log -S 'never runs\nahead of its parent'` names `ed864a9726` (a392) as the sole author of the GENERAL invariant\nsentence, and the same commit wrote both `_CASCADING_EDGES` and its \"exactly two cascading\nedges\" enumeration. Date test passes — the implicated lines do not predate the wrongness, they\nare it. Task 9788 (the original cascade) was considered and rejected: its ACs say \"Other\ntransitions (close, reopen, etc.) are unaffected\" and its I4a text was scoped to an `open`\nparent only, so this state did not violate anything before 2026-08-19. Exactly one `caused_by`\nedge is recorded (the explicit flag, not blame auto-derivation).\n\n**Scope & safety.** Caller sweep found no caller depending on the non-cascading behaviour. Test\ndiff is purely additive — zero deleted or weakened assertions. No new dependency.\n\n**Siblings.** Construct axis: none — the table is the single site. Parity axis (five-slot\ncensus of \"reactivating a closed ticket must reactivate closed ancestors\"): CLI and library go\nthrough `transition_compute` (covered); MCP `transition_ticket` delegates to `rebar.transition`\n(covered, no edit); `claim` is **N/A** — `txn.claim_core` refuses any status that is not `open`,\na deliberate refusal left untouched; **automated callers MISSING** — the reconciler writes\nSTATUS events directly (`apply_inbound_events.py:149,322`), bypassing the cascade seam on ALL\nthree edges, filed as `despotic-prime-reindeer` (bb73-97de-eeea-4899).\n\n**Residual risk.** One observed and dispositioned: an `open` parent is not eligible on this\nedge, so `child=in_progress, parent=open` stays reachable, and `blocked -> in_progress` cascades\nnothing although the start-work gate fires on it. Filed as `convenient-unrational-songbird`\n(0446-4278-b7df-438d). Both follow-ups linked `discovered_from` this bug.\n\n**Replication.** Fresh store, real CLI: parent and child both end `in_progress`. The reported\nstate no longer reproduces.\n\n**Gates.** lint PASS; typecheck Success (616 files); ratchet `new=0 increased=0 stale=0`;\n`tests/interfaces` 1967 passed; `tests/unit` subdirs 452 + 3231 passed. Two whole-`tests/unit`\nruns died at differing points (96%, then 43%) with exit 144 and ZERO failure markers — process\ntermination, not a test failure, so it was recorded rather than retried to green; the one\n`ERROR` in a chunk was the leak guard correctly catching a scratch file I had left in\nREPO_ROOT during the run, not a code defect. Authoritative sweep is CI's `Verified +1` on the\nmerged tree."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787315481266717001,"uuid":"eb368951-59cd-491e-b6fd-6c5a21b7e553"}